This confirms the issue is not with the CRI-O service itself. The problem is that the generated node networking configuration (20-nodenet.conf) contains a stale IPv6 address.

From your outputs:

ip -6 addr:

ens3f0: 2405:100:200:72::1:25

br-ex: 2405:100:200:72:50ed:ffbc:eea5:3135


But 20-nodenet.conf contains:

KUBELET_NODE_IP=2405:100:200:72:881e:df7b:f42e:9621
CONTAINER_STREAM_ADDRESS=2405:100:200:72:881e:df7b:f42e:9621


That address does not exist anymore.


---

This is most likely an OpenShift node IP detection problem

The file 20-nodenet.conf is generated automatically by OpenShift. It should never contain an address that is no longer assigned to the node.

Please run these commands next.

1. Check the current node IP annotations

cat /etc/kubernetes/kubelet-env

2. Check NetworkManager connection details

nmcli connection show
nmcli device show ens3f0

3. Check the node IPs known by the kernel

hostname -I
ip -4 addr
ip -6 route

4. From the bastion (or once the API is reachable)

oc get node sno1.lab1.ocp.lan -o yaml | grep -A20 addresses

and

oc get machineconfig 99-master-generated-kubelet -o yaml


---

Temporary recovery

If you need to get the SNO cluster back online immediately, you can temporarily update both generated files to use the actual IPv6 address (2405:100:200:72::1:25), then:

systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl restart crio
systemctl restart kubelet

This should allow CRI-O and kubelet to start, but remember that this is only a temporary workaround because these files are managed by the Machine Config Operator and will be regenerated.

What I suspect

Based on all the evidence, the reboot triggered by the MachineConfig update caused the host to come up with a different IPv6 address (likely because of SLAAC/DHCPv6), while the generated node configuration still references the old IPv6 address. That mismatch is preventing both CRI-O and kubelet from starting normally.

The outputs of kubelet-env, nmcli, and the Node object will tell us whether the stale IP came from Kubernetes, NetworkManager, or the generated MachineConfig.
